1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 We do have some bad news, which is Ian Massigan, who's the head of Applied AI at Anthropic.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 Unfortunately, he developed COVID symptoms yesterday and has been too sick to travel.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 The only good news is that means we're now back on schedule.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 So in terms of FinTech panel before lunch, we heard a lot of talk around things like sovereignty

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 and some of those concerns in terms of US Cloud Act and data sovereignty and where data gets shipped to

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 and what controls you can put in place.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 So with that, I'm delighted to announce our next speakers to tell us more around digital sovereignty

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 from theory to implementation.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
 So please put your hands together for Matt Johnson and Sharmayna Kanda from UBDS.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 Thanks, Matt.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 So great to be here again.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 This is my probably 15th year presenting.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 This is Sharmayna's first time as well.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 So hopefully we'll give you -- I've got the easy part.

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 I'm doing the sort of high-level overview and then Sharmayna's going to go into a bit more detail

16
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 around European sovereign cloud and so on.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 So just start with a very quick show of hands.

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 How many people here are familiar with the concept of digital sovereignty?

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 Am I going to come forward out of the glare of the lights?

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 Okay, cool.

21
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 Not bad.

22
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 So that's good.

23
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 How many of you are actively worried about it in your day-to-day role?

24
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 Okay, less.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 Fair enough.

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 Okay.

27
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 So this talk isn't going to preach to you.

28
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 It's not going to look at whether it's right or wrong.

29
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 It's going to give you some practical concerns related to sovereignty.

30
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 And it's going to ask the question, could your services still operate if access to a cloud

31
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 or an underlying technological service or environment changed?

32
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 Okay.

33
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 So let's start with some examples.

34
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 Four ways access can fail.

35
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 And four real-world events that's happened in the last few years that show the West.

36
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 This isn't just theory anymore.

37
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 So the first I'm sure everyone is familiar with is Ukraine.

38
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 So back in 2022 until about 11 days before the invasion, Ukraine had a whole bunch of laws

39
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 that said they weren't allowed to move their data outside of their borders.

40
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 And then they changed that just before the Russian invasion.

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 And a few of us, AWS and Microsoft and a bunch of other tech providers,

42
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 spent a few months helping them move some of their critical data out of region into the cloud.

43
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 And this gave them some protection because it's very difficult to see on the image there.

44
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 But if you see that red star, that is where a bomb landed.

45
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 The little blue dot just above it is their main data center that had all the government records in.

46
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 So it's 80 meters away from losing all their systems of record.

47
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
 Fast forward to March this year.

48
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 I'm not sure if anyone here has been operating AWS services in the Middle East.

49
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 If so, you've probably had a rough six months.

50
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 Again, with the war in Iran, or the conflict in Iran, what we found is that there's been a whole bunch of problems.

51
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 Facilities have been hit.

52
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 It's the first time that a hyperscale provider has been hit in this way.

53
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 And it's caused one region effectively to be rendered offline and the other is lost in full availability zone.

54
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 And so it's no longer just logical.

55
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 We've got the threat of drones breaking through the atmosphere and physically destroying data centers.

56
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 It's not just cyber attacks anymore.

57
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 And then this is probably the one that really got me thinking about sovereignty in detail.

58
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 Called Fable.

59
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 So tomorrow after June, US issued an emergency export control directive and told Anthropic to stop making that model available to anyone outside of the US,

60
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 including non-US citizens working for Anthropic.

61
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 That lasted probably about 12 days.

62
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 It was lifted in stages and so on.

63
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 But it's probably the first time since the 90s when, for those of us old enough to remember, PGP was restricted with export controls.

64
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:10,000
 It's been that long since we had a problem where the US government, or our government in general, has restricted broad access to a technology that people were using.

65
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 Now whether that impacted many people or not is open to debate, but you can also abstract that forward.

66
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 What happens if they turn off EntraID or they've restricted TLS in AWS?

67
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 So again, those concerns there.

68
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 And then, guess what?

69
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
 So this is as if we never learn, as we've been here before.

70
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 Yandex, literally just two weeks ago now.

71
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
 Ukraine responded by hitting a bunch of Yandex, so Russia data centers.

72
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 They're equivalent of Google.

73
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 Causing major problems to Russia's in-house or domestically ability to handle both searches and cloud provision and so on.

74
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 And actually, those of you who run web services probably noticed that the amount of bot traffic and malicious traffic dropped significantly for a few minutes as a result of that.

75
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 But again, it's the fact that sovereignty isn't an argument for one particular geography.

76
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
 And so I suppose what we're saying is that in-country is not a resilient strategy.

77
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 Some people say it was, and a lot of people when they talk about sovereignty think it needs to be inside borders.

78
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:16,000
 That's not what we're saying.

79
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 These incidents show that where it runs and who can determine whether it keeps running are distinct things.

80
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 And so, yeah, people think about in-country as owned infrastructure.

81
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 There's a derogatory term that people have used, tin huggers.

82
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
 I want to see my servers.

83
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,000
 I want to hug them.

84
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 That's great until somebody drops a bomb on them and then you're back to using pen and paper.

85
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 Domestic cloud is the next option.

86
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 And this is how many people have had the argument that we can only use it if we've got a region in country X.

87
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 You know, I remember the argument back in 2016, 2017 when AWS launched the London region for the first time.

88
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 It opened up a whole bunch of public sector workloads to be able to use that region they hadn't before.

89
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 To use AWS they hadn't been able to use before.

90
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 But as we've seen in the golfers, as we've seen in Russia, just having that domestic cloud capability is no good if you can take that out.

91
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
 And then the third area is around what we call imported technology.

92
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
 What happens when you're using technology that is under the control of another jurisdiction?

93
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
 So, again, it's a sign of the times when geopolitics needs to become part of the IT resilient strategy.

94
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:31,000
 And so the most important architectural lesson to take from this is to design for the threat model, not the label on the cloud itself.

95
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
 And it's worth pointing out that multi-region resilience is not sovereign resilience.

96
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 The design boundaries are different, the failover routines are different.

97
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
 It's really key to call on out.

98
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
 And Charmin will cover this in a bit more detail.

99
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 But as we all know, AWS regions have strong core isolation.

100
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 What happens in one region should not affect another region.

101
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
 And I think this has been proven multiple times.

102
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
 What happened in Bahrain didn't affect what happened in London or Ireland.

103
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
 What you'll find is there is still the common dependency boundary.

104
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
 So this still has a sort of a sovereign risk.

105
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:16,000
 AWS runs as a global partition, but that global partition is led out of US East 1, so North Virginia, which is in the US.

106
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
 The impacts there cause impacts to a bunch of services that are global or partition services.

107
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:30,000
 And if an impact from a technical perspective can have that issue, then a geopolitical impact could also have that impact as well.

108
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:35,000
 So again, multi-region resilience and sovereign failover are different things.

109
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 They're different fault boundaries.

110
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,000
 And the key thing is to identify which fault boundary matters most for your service.

111
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:47,000
 And I'm not saying that you need complete technological independence.

112
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
 So that's neither realistic, probably not desirable for most people's workloads.

113
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 You know, just doing multi-cloud is really challenging.

114
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
 Trying to do that while thinking about all the complexities of sovereignty is also an issue.

115
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 What we're looking for is what credible options remain if your circumstances change.

116
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,000
 Let's take a really bad hypothetical example.

117
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
 What happens if you lost entry ID?

118
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 Yeah, that would keep me up at night.

119
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
 It doesn't mean you have to fix it because the chances that happen are very low.

120
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 But you should at least start thinking where are your critical dependencies.

121
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 And we'll cover that a bit later on as well.

122
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,000
 So one way to look at it, and this is the way we've been working at UBDS as well,

123
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 is looking at what we call the eight dimensions of dependency.

124
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 So by that, what are the different aspects you can almost do a risk assessment on

125
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
 in terms of is this going to impact me?

126
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
 Is this a problem?

127
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 So you obviously have the technology itself.

128
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
 So, you know, where the technology is hosted, how it works, who owns it, that sort of thing.

129
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
 Where the data sits, how the data moves, who controls it, is encrypted.

130
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
 The operations often overlooked.

131
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:56,000
 You know, both for yourself, where your operators sit, but also your service providers.

132
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
 Is it a follow-the-sun model?

133
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
 Do they work in jurisdictions you understand?

134
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
 Security is a key one.

135
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 We've already talked about identity, but also things like key management,

136
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
 things like encryption, things like data transfer.

137
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
 People is another dimension that's often overlooked.

138
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
 Sovereign technology requires people not just to be able to operate it,

139
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
 but to understand it and have those people available to move.

140
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
 If you're dependent on a third-party provider, and you've outsourced all your knowledge,

141
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
 then that's a bad thing.

142
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
 Commercial and contracts are quite closely tied together.

143
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 So what you contractually allow to do, and what can you commercially afford to do?

144
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 Often two separate things.

145
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
 And the last one, I think this is the one that's become most relevant recently,

146
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
 is the jurisdiction.

147
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 How do we know that we can continue to operate services

148
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
 if another government, if another jurisdiction, decides they have problems with that?

149
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:52,000
 So really the unit of analysis is your critical service and its combined dependencies.

150
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 And of course, moving the code is hopefully the easy part.

151
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:05,000
 A service, a runnable service or a runnable workload is not the same as a fully recoverable service.

152
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:11,000
 So you can package an application up as a container, and hopefully you can run it in pretty much any environment.

153
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,000
 But again, as Sharmin is going to cover, how that service can be operated, all the things that wrap around it.

154
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 Whether that's data, DNS, identity, keys, CICD pipelines, deployment, operation support, and so on.

155
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
 And I think one of the key things there is a plan isn't an option until you've exercised it.

156
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 This is so often spoken about in terms of backups.

157
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 You know, a backup isn't a backup until you've proved you can restore it.

158
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
 Same thing with a disaster recovery or resilience plan.

159
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
 A plan's not there until you've had the option to exercise it.

160
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
 And again, sovereignty should be proportional to criticality.

161
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
 What we're not suggesting is go out and make everything completely cloud-agnostic.

162
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
 You know, lots of people have tried this in the past and invariably failed.

163
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
 It's about understanding what are your key things you need to think about.

164
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
 Where are your critical dependencies and how would you deal with them?

165
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
 So first, identify which services really matter.

166
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
 You've probably got a list of 100, 200, 300, even thousands of services.

167
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
 My guess is the ones that are truly critical to your business or operation are probably a dozen or so, maybe less than that.

168
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
 So start thinking about those.

169
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
 It doesn't mean you have to move them, you just need to understand them.

170
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
 What if we lose access to those?

171
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 If that was to go offline, either because of a technical issue, a legal issue, a jurisdictional issue, what would you do?

172
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 How would you deal with that?

173
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
 And so understand where those potential threats are.

174
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 Is it that the hardware provider might not be available?

175
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
 Is it that someone turns the network off?

176
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 And critically, can we operate this elsewhere?

177
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 Can we take that workload somehow and then move it somewhere to run?

178
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
 Now that is going to be challenging for a lot of people, especially if you've got cloud-native.

179
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:52,000
 Again, this isn't a pitch to go away and undo all the stuff that Julia was talking around, around service workloads and Lambda web functions.

180
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
 I'm looking forward to playing with those myself.

181
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:02,000
 What I'm saying is that be aware of those decisions you're making and be conscious of those decisions and think about what would happen if you needed to change that.

182
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 So my last slide, and then I'm going to hand over to Sharmin, is effectively what we're talking about now is what changes with the European Sovereign Cloud.

183
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
 Just a very quick show of hands, I've mentioned it a few times.

184
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
 Who are familiar with European Sovereign Cloud?

185
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 Okay, well Sharmin will cover it briefly, so I won't say too much about it.

186
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 What I will say is that it doesn't eliminate all of the risks.

187
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
 So we can have a great debate afterwards about whether it is truly sovereign, whether the US have any control over it.

188
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:38,000
 You know, we can get into great arguments.

189
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
 I used to work at AWS, I've got an opinion, people who work at AWS have an opinion, that's all fine.

190
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
 It's not about that, but this is potentially the closest thing you're ever going to get to what I would call a single vendor multi-pack.

191
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
 And I nick that from somebody else, but it's a great term.

192
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 In this, you can use all of the same tooling, you can use all of the same approaches, but you effectively have almost complete partitional resilience, which is quite unusual.

193
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
 So with that, I'm going to hand over to Sharmin, who's going to look a bit more in detail about European Sovereign Clash.

194
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:17,000
 So we've had a look at what the digital sovereignty means, and why it matters.

195
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
 So now I'm going to look at what that means, and what that changes technically.

196
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:30,000
 So on the left, we've got London with the commercial AWS cloud, and we've got the European Sovereign Cloud in Bradenburg, Germany.

197
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
 But this isn't about geography.

198
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 These aren't two regions.

199
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:41,000
 These are two different partitions with its own identity, own control plates, and own service endpoints.

200
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:47,000
 The separation gives operational independence, but that also comes with its complexities.

201
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:55,000
 We can't assume that everything's going to work as it would normally across two regions, because these are separate partitions.

202
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:02,000
 And the quick question would then be what needs to exist within these two different partitions.

203
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,000
 And within ESC, that would need to also be a question where we actually ask, as well, what needs to exist.

204
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
 So we're going to look at how we're going to achieve that.

205
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:28,000
 So we've got here a typical commercial workload with its users, applications, database, and object storage, and the supported services around that.

206
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
 And we also need to look at how do we actually maintain these data.

207
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 Is it that we're going to transfer it, or are we going to create them or copy them over?

208
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,000
 But then, at the same time, it depends on what the customer wants to use it for.

209
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,000
 Is it going to be a backup, is it a pilot live, or a warm, standby, active environment?

210
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
 Or is it just an application simply running in ESC?

211
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:06,000
 And here we've also got the ESC workload, with the users, and the object storage, and the databases as well, and all the tooling needed.

212
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:15,000
 But these all come with their own independent KBS keys, as well as their own identity and own users.

213
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:22,000
 So we can see that these are two different environments, but they all need to be created independently as well.

214
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:30,000
 So now we're going to look at what the things that we might need to recreate, or what we might need to copy.

215
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:38,000
 So we've got data, for example, we might want to transfer it, using a backup, export, or control to transfer.

216
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:44,000
 Application artifacts, such as container images and packages, that can also be transferred over.

217
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:49,000
 As well as infrastructure as code, where might this code actually sit?

218
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:03,000
